  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1236 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a closed upper low transitioning east through \nthe high plains of eastern Wyoming. Near the surface, a broad area \nof high pressure is shifting east through the Upper Midwest into the \nGreat Lakes Region.\n\nAnother round of thunderstorm chances (50-70%) return early in the \nperiod as the SREF indicates the closed upper low in eastern Wyoming \nedging eastward into western South Dakota/western Nebraska tonight,\nsetting up a series of H5 vort maxima ejecting eastward out of the\nColorado Rockies into the high plains of eastern Colorado. Near the\nsurface, a prevailing easterly upslope will reinforce pooled moisture\nacross the Western High Plains with surface dewpoints generally in\nthe lower/mid 60s(F), providing modest instability with MUCAPE values\nupward of 1000 to 1500 J/kg in an axis in extreme eastern Colorado\nnear and along the Kansas line. Thunderstorm development is likely\nlate afternoon into this evening as H5 vort maxima begin to eject\nout of the Rockies, interacting with a lee side trough axis/dryline\nin eastern Colorado, then becoming more numerous while spreading\neast-southeast into extreme western Kansas overnight in conjunction\nwith a compact +60kt jet core cycling through the westerly flow aloft.\nThis is suggested by CAMs and the HREF showing a 40-60% probability\nof 6-hr QPF exceeding 0.25 of an inch across a good portion of southwest\nKansas by early Monday morning. Similar conditions are forecast Monday\nevening with thunderstorm chances (20-30%) existing near and along\nthe Colorado line, though to a much lesser degree with a smaller\nscale upper level ridge axis beginning to amplify across the Western\nHigh Plains.\n\nMore seasonal temperatures are likely tonight as surface high pressure\ndrifting east through the Great Lakes helps reinforce a cooler air\nmass within a east-northeast upslope flow across the Western High\nPlains. With the latest HREF indicating a a 50-70% probability of\ntemperatures slipping below 60F in west central Kansas, central Kansas,\nand portions of extreme southwest Kansas, look for lows in the 50s(F)\nin our west/northwest with the lower 60s(F) likely in south central\nKansas where the probability of dropping below 60F is less than 10%.\nRelatively mild temperatures are forecast Monday as a prevailing cooler\nair mass is slow to erode within an easterly upslope flow, resulting\nin H85 temperatures reaching up around 15C in central/south central\nKansas to nearing 20C in extreme southwest Kansas. Expect afternoon\nhighs generally in the 70s(F) with the HREF indicating a 70-90%\nprobability of temperatures topping 75F across our east to a better\nthan 90% probability of exceeding 70F near and along the Colorado\nline.",
